onducted or funded by USDA and other agencies would be subject to these integrity standards."},{"industry":"education","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Universities receive federal research funding and would need to comply with integrity policies in their federally supported work."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require every federal agency that funds, conducts, or oversees scientific research to create and enforce a policy protecting scientific integrity. These policies must ensure that research conclusions are not influenced by political considerations, prohibit suppression or alteration of findings, and allow researchers to share their work. Each agency would need to appoint a Scientific Integrity Officer, establish a complaint process, provide training, and submit its policy to the White House Office of Science and Technology Policy for approval.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who uses a phone, the internet, or a connected device.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001225","score":0.04131484571444916},"overlapCount":131},"meaning":{"percentile":45.01658505765282,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6214506936969245}},{"billId":"119-hr-51","bioguideId":"R000599","cycle":2026,"score":0.008647770376139808,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":45000,"donorShare":0.025180272565818854,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.006867347063405142},{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":10000,"donorShare":0.005595616125737523,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.001780423312734666}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6214506936969245},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-51","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"51","title":"Washington, D.C. Admission Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Oversight and Government Reform, and in addition to the Committees on Rules, Armed Services, the Judiciary, and Energy and Commerce, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional districts and representation","Congressional elections","Constitution and constitutional amendments","District of Columbia","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","Government buildings, facilities, and property","House of Representatives","Legislative rules and procedure","Members of Congress","Presidents and presidential powers, Vice Presidents","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000147","cosponsorCount":204,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/51","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill fundamentally restructures the governance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":41.69957352708893,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Lawyers & Lobbyists is also the top donor sector for 2 other members from CA. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.6214506936969245}},{"billId":"119-hr-7977","bioguideId":"R000599","cycle":2026,"score":0.008056041451613287,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":43500,"donorShare":0.024340930146958224,"billConfidence":0.55,"contribution":0.00787500681225119},{"industry":"energy-renewable","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0005595616125737523,"billConfidence":0.55,"contribution":0.00018103463936209636}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"keyword-fallback","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6214506936969245},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-7977","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"7977","title":"Energy Bills Relief Act","introducedDate":"2026-03-18","latestActionDate":"2026-03-18","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on Agriculture, Ways and Means, Natural Resources, Financial Services, Transportation and Infrastructure, Education and Workforce, Oversight and Government Reform, and Science, Space, and Technology, for a period to be subsequ